@plane z=0 label="Context"
The spec stays `specs/cmd_change/cmd_change.spec.md`.

Exposes the single one-approval SpecSync lifecycle through equivalent human-readable and structured JSON commands under `specsync change`.

This plane is context. Later planes are lessons, in the order they were learned.
`context.md` stays beside this file.

@plane z=1 label="Lessons"
# Context

`change status` prints whether the pull request still has an open change and which one to archive first, without telling the operator to run `change status` again. `change ship` on a ready change commits the archive tip and then says to push and merge. It does not say to merge while another change is open. Re-running it on an archived change commits only that archive package.

`change pr` archives a ready change when it is the one to archive first, runs the pre-push gate, pushes the branch, and opens or reuses an open pull request. A change that is not ready still gets the pull request, then the one next command. A closed pull request is not reused. The command does not merge, and it does not say to merge while a change is still open. A change directory that holds a file but no `state.json` is still open and is told to run `change status`. An empty directory husk is not an open change.

`change new`, `change status`, and `change show` render the domain's open-change briefing in text and JSON. `change finalize` and `change ship` name the lesson-bundle path on its own line. Their next action stays the merge instruction.

The command layer intentionally contains no lifecycle policy, keeping agent and terminal behavior identical and the domain module independently testable. Its full text/JSON lifecycle passed end-to-end integration tests and project-level dogfooding for 5.0.

`change reopen` prints the domain `ReopenResult` directly in JSON so persisted and emitted audit metadata cannot drift. Human output states that fresh verification and closing approval are required.

`change correct` follows the same thin-dispatch rule. JSON emits the persisted correction, complete ordered history, effective definition, and summary; human correct/show/status output distinguishes original and effective values and names the next gate. All correction policy remains in `src/change.rs`.

`change correct-owner` also remains a thin adapter. It resolves repeated paths, a manifest, or `--all-missing` into domain entries, JSON emits the corrected persisted record, human output names the exact owner repair (or batch count) and next gate, and all path, ownership, state, and transactionality policy remains in `src/change.rs`.

`change review` parses the optional verdict through the typed change-domain enum. Text names the
stored verdict and JSON emits the exact review record; reviewer-claim validation, evidence
freshness, and finalization eligibility remain domain concerns. The reviewer MAY be the same
actor as definition approval. Next-action copy uses `--reviewer <human>` so the command layer
does not tell solo projects to invent a second identity. Reviewer text is a stable ASCII claim,
every attempt is append-only, and hosted required-check provenance supplies authenticated merge
trust. GitHub required reviews remain the two-person gate when a repository wants one.
Ship-status stage actions are a second copy site: updating `next_action` and the stale-review
blockers is not enough. After the domain gate allowed same-actor review, `ship_stages` still
emitted `--reviewer <other>` until Kyntrin's review of #749 named the leftover.

`answer`, `depend`, and `supersede` delegate correction-ledger health enforcement to their
mutation-capable domain operations, which reload and validate the ledger while holding the same
project lock used for persistence. Successful mutations return the validated effective definition
and correction history used for output; the command adapter retains live ledger validation only on
read-only rendering paths. This preserves the thin-dispatch boundary while preventing both the
lock-wait race and a false post-persistence failure. Human mutation output obtains its optional
correction counts from a separate best-effort `state.json` reload keyed by the original command ID;
the validated correction snapshot remains confined to JSON output so correction data cannot flow
into cleartext sinks.

Structured mutation rendering also selects the normal or explicit-strict summary captured by the
domain operation before it released the project lock. It never recomputes correction health from a
live ledger, so one response cannot contain a validated effective definition beside a contradictory
invalid-correction summary.

Each lifecycle verb builds its own next-action string. That is why `finalize` named the lesson
fold-back and `ship` did not: the same guidance had two authors, and only one was updated. The
archived bundle was written and nothing told the operator it existed — on the verb the tool
itself recommends. When guidance about the same step exists on more than one exit, extract it to
one pure function and pin it with a test; the regression is always a future edit to one verb that
forgets the other. `merge_before_finalize_warning` and `ship_next_action` are both that shape.

Surfacing affordances fail open and never gate: an unreadable context file yields no pointer
rather than an error. This is deliberately the opposite posture from evidence validation, which
fails closed throughout the domain. The distinction is whether the artifact is load-bearing for
trust or an aid to the author.

`ready_to_finalize` and `finalize` are two answers to one question, and the report is where they were
allowed to disagree. `review_present` asked `review.json.is_file()` while `finalize` required the
review to still be current, so ship-status recommended `specsync change ship` in the same second the
verb refused. #689 had already moved the verification half of that conjunction onto content and left
the review half asking whether a file existed — the same fix, one term short. When a status command
and the command it recommends both compute readiness, the status command must consult the *predicate*
the other one gates on, not a proxy for it; and the regression test must assert that the two AGREE
rather than that either returns a particular value, or it goes red the day the underlying policy is
decided the other way.

The existence-only question had three call sites inside one function, not one. Fixing only
`ready_to_finalize` would have left the `review_tip` stage reporting `done` for a review finalize
rejects and the text line printing `recorded` — and, because `archive_tip` is gated on
`ready_to_finalize`, it would have left NO stage marked `current`, which silently drops `ship_next`
back to the generic lifecycle sentence instead of naming the recovery. A report that has stopped
lying in its summary field and still lies in its stage list has not been fixed. `ChangeSummary` asked
the currency question correctly the whole time, which is the tell: when one projection of a value is
wrong, look for the sibling projections before concluding the defect is where it was reported.

Where a guarantee cannot be evaluated, the honest report is a third answer, not a rounded second one.
`unavailable` renders as `ready_to_finalize: false` plus a warning naming the re-review that
re-anchors it — not a blocker, because asserting that an unobtainable guarantee ought to block would
decide an open design question by accident. Readiness can decline to answer without also deciding
what should happen next.

Issue #745 extends the same verification-content predicate used by readiness to the product-tip stage. Commit ancestry remains diagnostic; it no longer substitutes for freshness in product-stage completion. The scoped-review currency check is unchanged, so unavailable review evidence may still prevent finalization even when product verification is current.

Lifecycle commits used to stage with `git add -A`, and `--push` published whatever that swept up:
a private debug archive in one project, an agent's `.agents/` directory and an experiment script in
another. `git_commit_lifecycle` now stages every tracked edit plus the untracked paths
`change::lifecycle_commit_scope` names, using explicit literal pathspecs, and lists every other
untracked file on stderr. Tracked edits stay in because verification digests the working tree, so
a tracked edit that was verified but not committed would leave CI checking a different tree.
Untracked files stay out because being on disk says nothing about belonging in history. A new
source file therefore joins the delivery when its author stages it. The warning says what removing
a left-out file does to verification, because the digest covers it where it sits. `run_checked_commit`'s
doc comment used to say nothing is committed unless verification passes. That holds for the first
pass only. When the second pass fails, the materialize commit stays on the branch rather than being
rewound, and the error now names it.

`text_mode_next_action` never asked whether the approval was current for an approved, implementing or verifying change. JSON already sent a stale scope to `change approve` while text `Next:` said `change check`. After #704 it said `check` for an edited approved delta as well, and `check` refuses both. The command layer still makes no lifecycle decision here. It asks the domain through `change::approval_current_for_guidance`, which is the predicate behind `summary.approval_valid` and returns only a boolean, so no digest reaches a text line. This is the sibling-projection lesson above once more: a text next action and a JSON next action are two projections of one value, and the fix had to reach both.

@plane z=2 label="Archive-first CI"
Ship guidance used to say: push the product tip, wait for trust and implementation ready, then push a review or archive tip that reuses the parent's green run. When CI became archive-first, that advice turned into a deadlock, because a tip with an open change now fails `Archive` and runs nothing else, so the parent it said to wait on can never go green. The guidance and the CI shape are one contract with two authors. Change both together, and point the live lookup at HEAD, where the only full run now happens. Stage ids stay stable so JSON consumers keep parsing. Only the words change.

@plane z=3 label="Approved delta declarations in JSON"

`approved_deltas` (REQ-cmd-change-023) is rendered verbatim from the domain, like `acceptance_entries`. `print_record` calls `change::approved_delta_summaries` inside the status and show read scope, so it shares the delta-binding read the `summary` above it used. `print_mutation_record` renders `DefinitionMutationResult.approved_deltas`, which the domain built under the project lock with the same memo as `summary`. It computes nothing after persistence. `change new` is the exception: it still renders through `print_record` outside any read scope, because `create_change` returns no mutation snapshot. Its list is always `[]` because a new change is a draft, so the docs say only that, and they claim the in-transaction build for `answer`, `depend` and `supersede` alone. 0xLeif amended REQ-cmd-change-023 to match on 2026-11-01: `change new --json` always carries an empty `approved_deltas`. The key is always present, so a consumer can tell an older binary (absent) from nothing to report (`[]`). Text output, `summary`, the roster and `change list --json` gain nothing. The values are module names, requirement IDs, section names, booleans and a parser diagnostic, so no digest reaches any sink.

@plane z=4 label="Disclosing what verification ignores"

Issue #825: `verification_ignored_paths` hides files from verification, so the command layer discloses it wherever it is in force. That means a `Verification ignores:` line and a standing note in `change status`, in `change check` and in the `--commit` path's own summary, and `summary.verification_ignored_paths` in JSON. All of it is omitted when the list is empty. An invalid entry is the text `Next:` and a `ship-status` blocker. The blocker replaces the stale-verification blocker, because `recorded_verification_is_current` turns the digest's refusal into `false`. The command layer decides nothing: it asks `change::verification_ignored_paths_refusal` and `verification_ignored_paths_for_display`, and the text carries entry paths and rule text, never a digest. `run_checked_commit` prints its own summary, so a disclosure added only to the plain check branch would have been missing from the path agents actually use.


@plane z=5 label="Historical receipt reconciliation"
A valid stored receipt does not prove that its old file bytes existed at a later definition base. Preserve ordinary before/after succession. An explicit historical scope must authenticate the complete anchored receipt, bind its frozen manifest and exact current input tuples, retain an unproven-transition classification in evidence and audit output, and require fresh scope approval, verification and review. Native archival preserves the original accepted-state bytes.

@plane z=6 label="Advertise only a command the state accepts"
The ship-status product stage used to say `change check <id> --commit` whenever verification was not current, whatever the state. `check` refuses a draft and an accepted or archived change, so the stage named a command that could only fail (#690). A stage action is a promise that the command will run. Before naming a verb, ask whether that verb's own state guard admits this state: a draft goes to `change approve`, and an accepted or archived change with stale evidence goes to `change reopen`, the audited way back. `product_stage_action` makes that choice, and a unit test drives every state through the stage and checks the verb against its guard. The stage id, readiness, `ship_next` and every gate stay as they were.

@plane z=7 label="An archived Next: line is not always empty"
The text `Next:` line printed `no further action` for every archived change, beside a JSON next action that named a restore for a corrupt archive (#880). `change ship-status` takes its archived next action from the same `text_mode_next_action`, so one fix reached both. The adapter still decides nothing: it asks `change::corrupt_archive_recovery_for_guidance`, which returns the domain's restore step (literals, the change ID and the package path, never a digest) or `None`.

@plane z=8 label="Every surface that names a next step asks the same question"
Issue #902. #889 taught `change review` and `change finalize` to refuse a copy the remote default had already archived, but two other surfaces kept their own idea of the next step: the pull-request sentence from `change status` and `change pr`, and the Archive job's `archive-readiness.sh`. Both still said `specsync change ship <id>` for a reviewed copy, which `ship` then refused. When a refusal is added to a command, find every place that advertises that command and give it the same test; a script that mirrors Rust logic needs its own test case that fails without the change, because nothing compiles the two together. The script answers only from refs already present and never fetches, so the Archive job's single-commit checkout cannot change its verdict.

@plane z=9 label="Recorded claims in status (#633)"
`change status` and `change show` read `change::recorded_claims` before printing. A relay record that cannot be read refuses the command with its recovery, rather than leaving the claim out. Text prints `Approval:` and `Review:` as recorded claims, with any `Relayed:` line underneath. Every recorded string passes through `printable_claim_text`, so a control character prints as its escape and cannot move or recolour the terminal. JSON adds `recorded_claims` only when there is one. An approve or review without relay flags still calls the original entry points, so those paths stay exercised and dead-code free.

@plane z=10 label="Readiness runs the refusing check (#986, #956)"
`ship-status` may only say "ready to finalize" when `ship` would finalize. Open tasks (#892) and canonical ownership (#986, #956) were both refusals that only finalize knew about. Each such refusal belongs in ship preflight as a blocker, and the next action names its recovery, not `change ship`.

@plane z=11 label="One answer command across refusal and status (#958)"
A single interview choice is a literal answer, not an angle-bracket placeholder: `constraints none` runs as printed, while `<none>` is shell input redirection. The domain owns one helper for approve's refusal and both status projections, including the scope-specific placeholders and the option to name constraining lessons or principles. Text status must recompute the draft's open questions itself; its caller may have an empty list even though `constraints` remains. When refusal guidance and status disagree, probe every refusal through both text and JSON and execute the command exactly as printed.
